  <AboutText>k__BackingField: '<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>

    Pyramid is a
    popular addition-based Solitaire game where the player must clear cards by adding
    them together to reach 13. It is played with a standard 52-card deck with 28
    cards arranged into a pyramid, with each card covered by two cards below it.


    The
    goal of Pyramid is to remove all cards from the titular pyramid. Cards can be
    removed in pairs if their values add up to 13 (Kings can be removed on their
    own). Cards cannot be removed if they are being covered by other cards. The game
    is over when no more cards can be removed from the pyramid, either due to it
    being empty or no more pairs being possible.


    <b><size=+10>History</size></b>

    Pyramid
    is part of a distinct branch of Solitaire games known as Addition and/or Pairing
    games. These games have been documented since the 19th century, but Pyramid is
    significantly younger, appearing in the 1949 book "The Complete Book of Solitaire
    and Patience Games" written by Albert H. Morehead and Geoffrey Mott-Smith.


    As
    Pyramid''s popularity grew, numerous variants were introduced to simplify the
    game, or to increase its incredibly low win rate (approximately 1 in 50 per the
    initial rules).


    <b><size=+10>Today</size></b>

    A variant of Pyramid
    called "Tut''s Tomb" was created for Microsoft Entertainment Pack 2 in 1990,
    which brought Pyramid into the digital landscape. Since then, Pyramid, alongside
    several variants, has only continued to grow in popularity. '
  <GuideText>k__BackingField: 
